  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1253 PM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nCan expect fairly warm and dry conditions today before a cold front \nmoves through the area sometime Tuesday evening. Rain chances will \nincrease into the morning hours with the best chance into the early \nafternoon as the front approaches. The highest chance for showers \nand thunderstorms will be confined more towards the north and \nnortheast across the Victoria crossroads(60-70%) with low to medium \nchances expected across the Coastal Plains and inner Brush \nCountry(30-50%). The stronger forcing and dynamics for strong to \nsevere thunderstorms will remain north of our area, but conditions \nwill still support some embedded strong storms with small hail and \ngusty winds. \n\nBeing that this front is pacific in nature, there won't be much of a \ncooldown. But nonetheless, we can expect cooler weather and lower \nhumidity with temperatures in the upper 70s to low 80s into \nWednesday. Temperatures will slowly creep back up into the 80s as we \ngo into Thursday with an increase in humidity as winds become from \nthe east to southeast, along with low to medium rain chances \nreturning as we go into Thursday evening. There are some models \nhinting on a potentially stronger cold front pushing through as we \ngo into the end of the weekend and into early next week, but \nconfidence is low at this time.",
